  [38;5;153mlogin()[39m returns [38;5;153m{ userId, token }[39m and stores nothing. [38;5;153mapp.js[39m decides what to persist, passing a storage adapter in. No
  module-level state anywhere.
  [3mTrade-offs:[0m the most testable and the easiest to reason about — every dependency is visible at the call site. But it
  pushes the work onto every consumer, and "works across the app" then means each form re-derives the ID itself. That's
  the thing you asked not to have.

  [1mMy recommendation: A, borrowing C's injection seam.[0m A gives you the single shared accessor you asked for; C's good
  idea is that the storage backend should be a constructor argument defaulting to [38;5;153msessionStorage[39m, so unit tests pass a
  plain object and need no DOM at all. That combination costs one extra parameter and buys dependency-free tests. B
  stays available later — you can add events to A's session module without changing any consumer that only reads.

  [1mTwo problems I need to flag before designing[0m

  [1m1. The token and the ID will have different lifetimes, and that's a bug waiting to happen.[0m You chose [38;5;153msessionStorage[39m
  for the ID and in-memory (or [38;5;153mHttpOnly[39m cookie) for the token. On refresh, the in-memory token vanishes but the ID
  survives — leaving the app holding a user ID with no credential, looking logged in while every API call fails. The
  clean resolutions are either (a) the backend sets an [38;5;153mHttpOnly[39m cookie so the session genuinely survives refresh, or (b)
  on load, an ID with no token is treated as logged-out and cleared. This needs deciding in the design, not after.

  [1m2. [38;5;153mpackage.json[39m has no [38;5;153m"type"[39m field, so [38;5;153msrc/*.js[39m is CommonJS.[0m Adding ES modules for the browser code means either
  setting [38;5;153m"type": "module"[39m and renaming those two Node files to [38;5;153m.cjs[39m, or giving the new browser files a [38;5;153m.mjs[39m extension.
  Not hard, but it touches files outside the feature.

  I'd also record as an explicit assumption: [1mthe login response shape is unknown[0m — nothing in this repo documents what
  [38;5;153mapi.example.com/login[39m returns. I'll write it as [38;5;153mAssumption: response is {userId, token}, validate via <the actual API
[39m  [38;5;153mcontract>[39m rather than as fact.

